    "comments": "bayurzx\nbayurzx\n\n4 years ago\nAzure Storage is a PaaS\nhttps://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/learn/modules/azure-networking-fundamentals/azure-virtual-network-fundamentals\nCheck Video At 1:14\nTelonious\nTelonious\n\n8 months, 1 week ago\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-azure-storage-services/2-accounts <---- here the direct link :D ;)\nEliasMartinelli\nEliasMartinelli\n\n9 months, 1 week ago\nTrue comment\nCommunity vote distribution\nB (57%)\nA (43%)\n\nvishamay\nvishamay\n\n8 months, 1 week ago\nAzure Storage Services can be categorized under both IaaS and PaaS, depending on the use case.\nThey are IaaS since Azure Storage Services provide the fundamental building blocks for cloud-based applications and services,\nwhere you get raw storage capacity (like a hard drive in the cloud) and are responsible for how you manage and utilize that\ncapacity.\nThey are PaaS since Azure handles all the infrastructure management, maintenance, and scalability, allowing users to focus on\nhow they utilize the storage rather than managing the underlying hardware or OS.\nBut according to the old definition and used cases you think most if the services under Azure Storage Services can be count\nunder IaaS but some of them are more aligned with PaaS definiton.\nMassy\nMassy\n\n4 years ago\nI agree, Azure Storage is Paas\nAVP_Riga\nAVP_Riga\n\n3 years, 9 months ago\nThanks!\naaasdf\naaasdf\n\n4 years ago\nheh, you even pasted the link... storage is IaaS so the answer is correct\nhttps://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/overview/what-is-iaas/\nEGEmon\nEGEmon\n\n2 days, 11 hours ago\nSelected Answer:\nB\nWhile Azure App Service is a PaaS offering, Azure Storage accounts, while having some PaaS-like qualities, are generally\nconsidered IaaS.\nTherefore, using Azure Storage accounts directly does not meet the requirement of usin",
