    "comments": "DerelictX\nDerelictX\n\n4 years, 3 months ago\nCorrect answer is D - Hybrid cloud. Utilize current resources and dynamically scale in public cloud if and when needed.\nSadioMane\nSadioMane\n\n3 years, 8 months ago\nAnswer is D.\nFirst, The question is not asking to get ride of the already existing on-premise server. Second, we need to optimize the service as\nwell as cutting the cost. So using little bit of the cloud (cheaper) in addition to the physical servers we have. which is Hybrid\napproach.\nSisb\nSisb\n\n2 years, 1 month ago\nTotally agree. In ERP industry, a lot of end user has own on- premise server. For taking best of old server, they can not get ride\nof server right now, but they still want some cloud feature and function, so they pick Hybrid.\ngabrisiq\ngabrisiq\n\n1 year, 7 months ago\nHe already said the answer is D\nCommunity vote distribution\nD (90%)\nA (10%)\n\nHe already said the answer is D\ncherrada\ncherrada\n\n4 years, 3 months ago\nIf you need additional resources the better option is a Hybrid cloud with minimal CAPEX/OPEX\nGianlucag77\nGianlucag77\n\n3 years, 9 months ago\nadditional resources \"to users\", I think the answer is C\nm1nt1w0p4cc\nm1nt1w0p4cc\n\n2 years, 8 months ago\nwrong... you have to reduce CapEx to a minimum, that's not accomplished with private cloud... best solution is to co-exists both\ninfrastructures for the company (private + public = Hybrid)... that's why the correct answer is hybrid\nptjuanramos\nptjuanramos\n\n2 years, 10 months ago\nHow hosting a private cloud could increase the additional resources and keep on track de CapEx expending's?\nUmesh919\nUmesh919\n\n1 month, 4 weeks ago\nSelected Answer:\nD\nHybrid Cloud\nintentando\nintentando\n\n3 months ago\nSelected Answer:\nD\nhttps://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/solutions/hybrid-cloud-app/\nGoodman_5\nGoodman_5\n\n7 months ago\nBalanc",
