    "id": 54,
    "topic": 1,
    "type": "drag-drop",
    "question": "DRAG DROP -\nMatch the Azure services benefits to the correct descriptions.\nInstructions: To answer, drag the appropriate benefit from the column on the left to its description on the right. Each benefit\nmay be used once, more than once, or not at all.\nNOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.\nSelect and Place:",
    "options": [],
    "answer": [],
    "answerText": "Box 1:\nFault tolerance is the ability of a service to remain available after a failure of one of the components of the service.\nFor\nexample, a service running on multiple servers can withstand the failure of one of the servers.\nBox 2:\nDisaster recovery is the recovery of a service after a failure. For example, restoring a virtual machine from backup after a\nvirtual machine failure.\n\nvirtual machine failure.\nBox 3:\nDynamic scalability is the ability for compute resources to be added to a service when the service is under heavy load.\nFor\nexample, in a virtual machine scale set, additional instances of the virtual machine are added when the existing virtual\nmachines are under heavy load.\nBox 4:\nLatency is the time a service to respond to requests.\nFor example, the time it takes for a web page to be returned from a\nweb server.\nLow latency means low response time which means a quicker response.\nReferences:\nhttps://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/magazine/mt422582.aspx\nhttps://searchdisasterrecovery.techtarget.com/definition/cloud-disaster-recovery-cloud-DR http://www.siasmsp.com/the-\nbenefit-of-scalability-in-cloud-computing-2/ https://azure.microsoft.com/en-in/overview/what-is-cloud-computing/",
    "comments": "panal\npanal\n\n2 years, 6 months ago\ncorrect\nKanders\nKanders\n\n6 months, 3 weeks ago\nDisaster Recovery = Cloud service that can be recovered after a failure.\nFault Tolerance = Cloud service that remains available after a failure.\nLow latency = Cloud service that can be access quickly from internet.\nDynamic scalability = Cloud service that performs quickly when demand increases.\ntrashbox\ntrashbox\n\n3 weeks, 6 days ago\n\"remains available after a failure\" => Fault tolerance\n\"recovered after a failure occurs\" => Disaster recovery\n\"when demands increases\" => Dynamic scalability\n\"can be accessed quickly\" => Low latency\nRagnarLodbrok\nRagnarLodbrok\n\n6 months, 2 weeks ago\ncorrect\nbuiducvu\nbuiducvu\n\n8 months ago\nFault tolerance\nDisaster recovery\nDynamic scalability\nLow Latency\nPa1theAchiever\nPa1theAchiever\n\n1 year, 1 month ago\ncorrect\nkapy024\nkapy024\n\n1 year, 2 months ago\nCorrect\nSwathiraja\nSwathiraja\n\n1 year, 2 months ago\nThank you all\ncuentaalternajsr\ncuentaalternajsr\n\n1 year, 2 months ago\n\ncuentaalternajsr\ncuentaalternajsr\n\n1 year, 2 months ago\nCorrecto!\nBabuMaddineni\nBabuMaddineni\n\n1 year, 5 months ago\nCorrect\nnav777\nnav777\n\n1 year, 7 months ago\nCorrect. FT, DR, Dynamic Scalability & Low Latency\nngin\nngin\n\n1 year, 10 months ago\ncorrect!!!\nbeginnercloud\nbeginnercloud\n\n2 years, 2 months ago\ncorrect\nKemie2\nKemie2\n\n2 years, 2 months ago\nThank you\nfor the confirmation\nand clarifications of the answers.\nsachin\nsachin\n\n2 years, 3 months ago\nI think it should be fault tolerance, disaster recovery, dynamic scalability and low latency\nniccololepri\nniccololepri\n\n2 years, 2 months ago\none way to distinguish them is that 'fault tollerance' relates to the time-interval in which the system is not up, while 'disaster\nrecovery' relates to the time-interval after the system has been brought back u",
