    "comments": "Super63\nSuper63\n\n4 months ago\nThe answer clearly is HYBRID. There is a mix of \"on-premises\" and \"cloud base\" resources.\nNote similar linked/related\nquestions: 47, 48, 51, 55,60,61,62,63,85\nanakinr\nanakinr\n\n11 months ago\nSelected Answer:\nA\nHybrid\nwsjones\nwsjones\n\n1 year, 4 months ago\nThis showed up today. Had 36 questions and scored an 857, and the community and questions here were all covered on the test!\nhcq31818\nhcq31818\n\n1 year, 7 months ago\nSelected Answer:\nA\nHybrid\nKishu1987\nKishu1987\n\n1 year, 8 months ago\nHybrid\nCommunity vote distribution\nA (100%)\n\nStasheck\nStasheck\n\n2 years, 4 months ago\nSelected Answer:\nA\nIn general, on-prem is not the same as private cloud - as per MS, you can actually have private cloud deployed from some other\nvendor datacenter (not necessarily \"on-prem\" per se). However, in many questions regarding hybrid model \"on-prem\"+\"public\ncloud\" = hybrid, on-prem is not \"cloud\" per se, but it's private, and private + public cloud => hybrid.\nminigold\nminigold\n\n2 years, 5 months ago\nI am always confused ...\n1. Question said\n“Which cloud computing model …”, it means that answer [A. hybrid] is [hybrid cloud computing model].\n2. Question said\n“… includes on-premises & cloud-based resources”\n, then it means [on-premises] is not cloud service.\nTherefore, “hybrid cloud = [On-premises (not cloud)] + [cloud-based resources (cloud)] “ is right? I don't think so, on-premise is\nnot cloud.\nI think [on-premise] & [private cloud] are different, but this question deals both as same thing. Which point am I making a\nmisunstanding?\nJT19760106\nJT19760106\n\n2 years, 5 months ago\nShort answer, yes you are misunderstanding.",
