    "comments": "Thyfere\nThyfere\n\n5 years, 4 months ago\nServices are being discussed. Not all regions offer the same services; therefore, answer is B\nfspellet\nfspellet\n\n3 years, 10 months ago\n\"You need to ensure that the services running on the virtual machines are available if a single data center fails.\"\nThis sentence implies that while you're setting up the service, the regions you have chosen already have the services you need.\nRegardless if all regions offer the same services, you wouldn't use 2 regions that didn't have all the services available to you, thus\nmaking the question irrelevant if that was the case.\nMIU\nMIU\n\n3 years, 9 months ago\n>You plan to deploy several Azure virtual machines.\nThis is just a simple case for \"Deploying Azure virtual machines\".\nCommunity vote distribution\nA (72%)\nB (28%)\n\nThis is just a simple case for \"Deploying Azure virtual machines\".\nAny region has this fundamental function....So the answer is \"Yes\".\nsamraw83\nsamraw83\n\n2 years, 7 months ago\nif datacenter is the scope, it only needs availability zone.\nalisag09\nalisag09\n\n4 years, 1 month ago\nif the region did not offer a particular service, you anyway cannot create a resource. isn't it?\nAmit0807\nAmit0807\n\n5 years ago\nBut the solution says you have deployed it in multiple regions hence it can be assumed that the services were available in other\nregions\nBond\nBond\n\n5 years, 4 months ago\ni think what is required is best possible answer.. regions are NOT primarily designed for fault tolerance.\nshishal\nshishal\n\n4 years, 4 months ago\nWe may be overdoing it but regardless it meets the goal. May not be very cost effective or performant but that's not the goal here.\n[Removed]\n[Removed]\n\n2 years, 11 months ago\nAgree. It meets the goal. This is literally what happened with AWS when the entire East region went d",
