    "comments": "TheSwedishGuy\nTheSwedishGuy\n\n3 years, 11 months ago\nI can't believe there has been 101 replies to this comment section and not one clear answer.\nSo let me provide it easily for you guys:\nThe computer has PowerShell Core 6.0, but it doesn't have the Azure CLI installed. There is no mention of that in the question.\nYou need Azure PowerShell module in addition to PowerShell to run Azure commands, such as New-AzVM.\nTherefore, the answer should be B, No.\nMole857\nMole857\n\n9 months, 2 weeks ago\nAgreed - Just Powershell Core 6.0 is not sufficient. The Azure PowerShell module also needs to be installed.\nChandu2259\nChandu2259\n\n1 year, 8 months ago\nCommunity vote distribution\nB (60%)\nA (40%)\n\nPowerShell 7.0.6 LTS and PowerShell 7.1.3 or higher is the recommended version of PowerShell for use with the Az PowerShell\nmodule on all platforms.\njosephbark\njosephbark\n\n3 years, 4 months ago\nYou don't need Azure CLI to run Powershell script.\nwangyindong\nwangyindong\n\n2 years, 8 months ago\nthen how can you create azure resources which asked in the question?\nsdokmak\nsdokmak\n\n2 years, 11 months ago\nyup, you don't need Azure CLI tools to script Azure VM deployments in powershell, although would be easier. And even if you\nwanted to, you can still include the azure cli installation and imports as part of the script.\n[Removed]\n[Removed]\n\n3 years, 7 months ago\nTack så mycket!\nNujjy\nNujjy\n\n4 years, 9 months ago\nThese questions are poorly worded. You should still need to install the Az module inside powershell core for this to run.\ndouivayl\ndouivayl\n\n3 weeks, 6 days ago\nSelected Answer:\nB\nIf you are reading this and wondering about the discussion - don't read this one anymore, it is a bunch of nonsense. Go to the next\nquestion.\nPN60\nPN60\n\n3 months, 4 weeks ago\nThe answer is No because its not a strai",
