    "comments": "hgx32983\nhgx32983\n\n4 years, 2 months ago\nQuestion saying there should be 2 answer, not just one as given in the solution.\nShould be B (NSG) and D (Firewall)\nCyberAmit\nCyberAmit\n\n4 years, 2 months ago\nB+D are the correct answers\nchikkz\nchikkz\n\n3 years, 6 months ago\nCorrect\nNathan12345\nNathan12345\n\n6 months ago\nSelected Answer:\nB\nthis is for individual or small scale.\nfirewall is for large scale, network wide control.\nCommunity vote distribution\nB (65%)\nD (35%)\n\nfirewall is for large scale, network wide control.\nKholio86\nKholio86\n\n7 months, 2 weeks ago\nB & D are the correct answers\nsiculoct\nsiculoct\n\n8 months, 3 weeks ago\nB and D\nSAFM\nSAFM\n\n1 year, 4 months ago\nSelected Answer:\nB\ncomment does not allow multiple selections (please change this!)\nB. Modify a network security group (NSG)\nD. Modify an Azure firewall\nMANANDAVEY\nMANANDAVEY\n\n1 year, 7 months ago\nSelected Answer:\nB\nB. Modify a network security group (NSG)\nD. Modify an Azure firewall\nExplanation:\nTo ensure that a virtual machine named VM1 is accessible from the Internet over HTTP, you can use the following two solutions:\nB. Modify a network security group (NSG): An NSG is a fundamental Azure resource that allows or denies network traffic to and\nfrom Azure resources, such as virtual machines. By default, incoming traffic to a virtual machine is blocked. To allow incoming\nHTTP traffic (port 80), you need to create an NSG rule that permits inbound traffic on port 80 for the specific virtual machine (VM1).\nD. Modify an Azure firewall: Azure Firewall is a cloud-based network security service that provides stateful firewall capabilities. If\nyou are using Azure Firewall to protect your virtual network, you need to create a rule that allows HTTP traffic (port 80) from the\nInternet to the specific virtual machine (VM1).\nBlu",
