    "comments": "Look4you\nLook4you\n\n4 years, 9 months ago\nThe correct answer should be removing the 10 IP addresses\n[Removed]\n[Removed]\n\n2 years, 11 months ago\nCommunity vote distribution\nB (100%)\n\nwrong. the answer is B. Read the question carefully\nRajaRao\nRajaRao\n\n4 years, 11 months ago\nCorrect. Removal of unused groups will have no implications to the cost.\nSuper63\nSuper63\n\n3 months, 3 weeks ago\nThe first job in any Microsoft question is to properly interpreting the question as some are vague or even downright missleading.\nThis question is not asking for the correct answer- it is asking whether \"removing unused groups\" would reduce costs. The\nAnswer to this is NO as removing groups does not reduce costs\nzellck\nzellck\n\n2 years, 1 month ago\nSelected Answer:\nB\nB is the answer.\nzellck\nzellck\n\n2 years, 1 month ago\nRemoving the unused groups in Azure AD will not significantly reduce the Azure costs for the company, as groups are a low-cost\nresource in Azure.\nTo reduce Azure costs, you should consider removing or deallocating other unused resources that are more costly, such as the\n20 user accounts in Azure AD, the 10 public IP addresses, and the 10 network interfaces. Removing or deallocating these\nresources can help to reduce the Azure costs for the company.\nTonyghostR05\nTonyghostR05\n\n2 years, 4 months ago\nYou need to read the question carefully. This user is using subscription plan, therefore, delete any group is no effect about the\ncost.\nCorrect Ans is remove public IP address\niedodo\niedodo\n\n2 years, 11 months ago\nSince AD is tied to licences, both the idea of removing unused Groups and Account won't impact the cost, meaning the IP\naddresses still sounds the best option\nAzurite\nAzurite\n\n4 years ago\nGot this ? as a radio button instead of several questions on Jan 24, 2021\nkjon16\nkjon16",
