    "comments": "siculoct\nsiculoct\n\n8 months, 3 weeks ago\nIts NYY\nCustomer Responsibilities:\nApplication Code: The customer is responsible for developing, deploying, and maintaining the application code that runs on\nthe Azure Web App. This includes writing and updating the code, testing it for compatibility and functionality, and deploying it\nto the Azure Web App.\nConfiguration Management: The customer configures settings and parameters for the Azure Web App, such as scaling settings,\n\nConfiguration Management: The customer configures settings and parameters for the Azure Web App, such as scaling settings,\ncustom domain names, SSL certificates, and application settings.\nData Management: The customer manages the data used by the application, including databases, storage accounts, and other\ndata sources. This includes data backups, replication, and compliance with data protection regulations.\nPerformance Optimization: The customer monitors and optimizes the performance of the application running on the Azure\nWeb App, including performance tuning, troubleshooting, and scaling based on demand.\nPPV20\nPPV20\n\n11 months, 1 week ago\nNYY is the answer\nrankocertified\nrankocertified\n\n3 months, 2 weeks ago\n@Moderator, it is obvious the second answer is Y, please update the official answer\nPN60\nPN60\n\n3 months, 4 weeks ago\nNYY\nis correct. Co Pilot for the differing answer choice - In a Platform as a Service (PaaS) model like Azure Web Apps, the\ncustomer is responsible for managing and updating the application code. Azure handles the underlying infrastructure, OS\nupdates, and scaling, which means you can focus on developing and maintaining your application without worrying about the\nhardware and software layers below.\nd834f0c\nd834f0c\n\n5 months, 4 weeks ago\nVirtual network is IaaS\nDhinesh58\nDhinesh58\n\n1",
